1
00:00:00,219 --> 00:00:04,213
Every morning, Sarah starts with the
same question, what's running low?

2
00:00:04,643 --> 00:00:08,381
It sounds like a simple question,
but for Sarah, answering it takes

3
00:00:08,421 --> 00:00:09,511
around 40 minutes.

4
00:00:10,326 --> 00:00:15,036
She opens Odoo, exports a stock list,
finds last week's Excel file with

5
00:00:15,076 --> 00:00:19,283
the reorder points, runs a VLOOKUP,
fixes the rows that don't match,

6
00:00:19,443 --> 00:00:22,728
highlights the products that are
running low and finally sends Tom a

7
00:00:22,768 --> 00:00:26,900
list. And by the time the list reaches
the warehouse, another 200 boxes

8
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:28,465
might already have gone out.

9
00:00:28,505 --> 00:00:31,983
40 minutes a day, more than 3
hours every week just to answer one

10
00:00:32,023 --> 00:00:35,962
question. So what if Sarah could
simply ask that question and get an

11
00:00:36,002 --> 00:00:38,819
answer in seconds. That's what
we are going to build today.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,298
We are creating Stocky version 1.

13
00:00:41,338 --> 00:00:45,009
If you have been following along
since video 1, you already know Stocky

14
00:00:45,049 --> 00:00:48,761
as our AI intern. So far, we have
talked about what it should do.

15
00:00:48,801 --> 00:00:52,851
We have prepared the ERP, organized
the data and set up the tools.

16
00:00:52,891 --> 00:00:54,821
Now we are finally connecting everything.

17
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,055
Stocky will have a chat window
where Sarah can ask questions,

18
00:00:58,095 --> 00:01:01,476
a brain powered by Claude, a little
memory to follow the conversation,

19
00:01:01,516 --> 00:01:05,393
and most importantly, tools that let
it read live information from Odoo.

20
00:01:05,673 --> 00:01:09,850
So Sarah can type what's running low,
and Stocky will check the products

21
00:01:09,890 --> 00:01:13,503
against their reorder points and
return the results in a clear table.

22
00:01:13,563 --> 00:01:17,283
But I don't want you to just watch
an answer appear on screen and take

23
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:21,551
it on trust. I will also show you
how to open the execution log and

24
00:01:21,591 --> 00:01:25,497
follow what STOKI actually did, which
tool it called, what information

25
00:01:25,537 --> 00:01:29,127
came back from Odoo and how it
used the information to answer.

26
00:01:29,245 --> 00:01:32,865
Because in supply chain, you need
to be able to trace a number back to

27
00:01:32,905 --> 00:01:36,440
its source. And there's one more
important thing that we will cover

28
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:40,481
hallucinations. That's when an
AI give you an answer that sounds

29
00:01:40,611 --> 00:01:43,081
convincing but is not
supported by the facts.

30
00:01:43,144 --> 00:01:46,907
We will deliberately catch Stocky
doing that, then improve its

31
00:01:46,947 --> 00:01:49,997
instructions so it knows
when to say "I don't know".

32
00:01:50,037 --> 00:01:53,493
If Video 5 was about setting up
the desk and getting the keys,

33
00:01:53,533 --> 00:01:55,902
this is the day our intern
finally starts work.

34
00:01:55,942 --> 00:01:57,122
Let's build Stocky.

35
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:01,125
Open n8n and click "Create Workflow".

36
00:02:01,738 --> 00:02:05,788
Name it straight away:
"Stocky v1, Reads the ERP".

37
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:09,813
This is the workflow you'll keep
improving for the rest of the course.

38
00:02:11,010 --> 00:02:14,035
Click "Add first step" and
choose "On chat message".

39
00:02:14,648 --> 00:02:16,998
Last video our doorbell was a button.

40
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:20,260
Today, the doorbell is a chat message.

41
00:02:20,873 --> 00:02:24,385
Every time someone types a
question, the recipe starts.

42
00:02:24,998 --> 00:02:27,185
See the new "Chat" button at the bottom?

43
00:02:27,797 --> 00:02:29,573
That's where we'll talk to Stocky.

44
00:02:30,780 --> 00:02:34,043
Now click the plus, search
"AI Agent", and add it.

45
00:02:34,655 --> 00:02:36,293
Look at "Source for Prompt".

46
00:02:36,905 --> 00:02:39,668
It already says "Connected
Chat Trigger Node".

47
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:45,118
Exactly right. Whatever we type
in the chat becomes Stocky's task.

48
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:46,868
Close the panel.

49
00:02:48,054 --> 00:02:51,329
Underneath the AI Agent,
remember the three plugs.

50
00:02:51,942 --> 00:02:57,114
First, the brain. Click the plus
under "Chat Model", choose "Anthropic

51
00:02:57,154 --> 00:03:00,989
Chat Model", pick the credential
"Anthropic, BrightLeaf" we saved last

52
00:03:01,029 --> 00:03:03,767
time, and choose the
newest Claude Sonnet model.

53
00:03:04,379 --> 00:03:07,627
Sonnet is a great choice for agents
because it's good at deciding which

54
00:03:07,667 --> 00:03:08,854
tool to use.

55
00:03:10,056 --> 00:03:11,631
Second plug: memory.

56
00:03:12,243 --> 00:03:15,218
Click the plus under "Memory"
and choose "Simple Memory".

57
00:03:15,831 --> 00:03:17,269
Why do we need this?

58
00:03:17,881 --> 00:03:21,368
Without memory, Stocky forgets
everything after each message.

59
00:03:21,981 --> 00:03:27,279
You ask about BL-1005, then ask "and
who supplies it?", and Stocky says,

60
00:03:27,319 --> 00:03:31,256
"Supplies what?" Like an
intern with a goldfish brain.

61
00:03:31,868 --> 00:03:35,279
The "Context Window Length" is how
many back-and-forth messages Stocky

62
00:03:35,319 --> 00:03:38,156
remembers. I'll set it to ten.

63
00:03:38,769 --> 00:03:41,106
That's plenty for a normal conversation.

64
00:03:42,306 --> 00:03:45,181
Before we go further,
let's test the skeleton.

65
00:03:45,794 --> 00:03:49,566
Click "Chat" and ask: "Hi Stocky,
how many Pallet Jack Wheels do we

66
00:03:49,606 --> 00:03:52,194
have?" Look at the answer.

67
00:03:52,806 --> 00:03:56,231
Stocky politely says it doesn't
have access to inventory data.

68
00:03:56,843 --> 00:04:01,381
And that's good! It has a brain
and a memory, but no hands.

69
00:04:01,993 --> 00:04:03,944
It can't reach into Odoo yet.

70
00:04:04,556 --> 00:04:07,206
A brain without tools is just a chatbot.

71
00:04:07,819 --> 00:04:09,243
Let's give it hands.

72
00:04:11,767 --> 00:04:15,339
Quick check in Odoo first, because
an agent is only as good as the data

73
00:04:15,379 --> 00:04:20,617
it reads. Here's BL-1005,
Pallet Jack Wheels.

74
00:04:21,229 --> 00:04:26,202
On hand: eighteen. The reorder
rule, here in the "Min Max" button:

75
00:04:26,242 --> 00:04:27,792
minimum twenty-five.

76
00:04:28,404 --> 00:04:32,389
And on the Purchase tab, the vendor:
Global Lift Parts, thirty-eight

77
00:04:32,429 --> 00:04:35,792
dollars a set, delivery
lead time twenty-one days.

78
00:04:36,404 --> 00:04:39,777
Three kinds of information, in three
different drawers of the filing

79
00:04:39,817 --> 00:04:44,579
cabinet. So we'll give Stocky
three tools, one for each drawer.

80
00:04:45,192 --> 00:04:48,689
If one of your products is missing
a reorder rule or a vendor line,

81
00:04:48,729 --> 00:04:52,267
add it now. Garbage in, garbage out.

82
00:04:53,473 --> 00:04:56,335
Back in n8n, click the plus under "Tool".

83
00:04:56,948 --> 00:04:59,235
Search "Odoo" and choose "Odoo Tool".

84
00:04:59,848 --> 00:05:03,658
This is the same Odoo waiter we met
in Video 3, but now it works for the

85
00:05:03,698 --> 00:05:08,248
agent. Stocky can call it
whenever it decides it needs data.

86
00:05:09,451 --> 00:05:11,538
Let's set it up line by line.

87
00:05:12,151 --> 00:05:16,438
First, rename the node to "get
products and stock", with underscores.

88
00:05:17,051 --> 00:05:20,423
The name matters, because Stocky
reads it to decide when to use this

89
00:05:20,463 --> 00:05:24,326
tool. Credential: "Odoo, BrightLeaf".

90
00:05:24,938 --> 00:05:26,926
Next, "Tool Description".

91
00:05:27,538 --> 00:05:31,086
Set it manually and describe in
plain English what this tool returns:

92
00:05:31,126 --> 00:05:36,001
every product, with its SKU, name,
quantity on hand and unit cost.

93
00:05:36,613 --> 00:05:39,038
This sentence is like
the label on the drawer.

94
00:05:39,651 --> 00:05:42,651
A clear label means Stocky
opens the right drawer.

95
00:05:43,837 --> 00:05:46,487
Resource: choose "Custom Resource".

96
00:05:47,099 --> 00:05:49,262
That lets us pick any drawer in Odoo.

97
00:05:49,874 --> 00:05:52,612
For the name, choose
"product dot product".

98
00:05:53,224 --> 00:05:55,374
Odoo might call it "Product Variant".

99
00:05:55,987 --> 00:05:58,187
That's the list of
products with their stock.

100
00:05:58,799 --> 00:06:01,987
Operation: "Get Many", because
we want the whole list.

101
00:06:02,599 --> 00:06:04,224
Switch on "Return All".

102
00:06:04,837 --> 00:06:08,237
And finally, under Options,
"Fields to Include".

103
00:06:08,849 --> 00:06:12,634
We'll pick just four fields: default
code, which is Odoo's name for the

104
00:06:12,674 --> 00:06:16,824
SKU, name, quantity
available, and standard price.

105
00:06:17,437 --> 00:06:21,872
Why only four? Because every extra
field is more reading for Stocky,

106
00:06:21,912 --> 00:06:25,849
which means more tokens, more
cost, and more chance of confusion.

107
00:06:26,462 --> 00:06:29,624
Give the intern the page they
need, not the whole binder.

108
00:06:30,823 --> 00:06:32,610
Tool number two is quicker.

109
00:06:33,223 --> 00:06:36,210
Duplicate the first one
and change three things.

110
00:06:36,823 --> 00:06:39,060
The name: "get reorder rules".

111
00:06:39,673 --> 00:06:43,658
The drawer: "stock dot warehouse dot
orderpoint", which Odoo may show as

112
00:06:43,698 --> 00:06:45,285
"Minimum Inventory Rule".

113
00:06:45,898 --> 00:06:50,273
And the fields: product, minimum
quantity, and maximum quantity.

114
00:06:50,885 --> 00:06:53,510
The minimum quantity
is your reorder point.

115
00:06:54,123 --> 00:06:56,623
Update the description
to say exactly that.

116
00:06:57,817 --> 00:07:00,779
And tool number three:
"get supplier info".

117
00:07:01,392 --> 00:07:05,277
The drawer is "product dot supplier
info", which may show as "Supplier

118
00:07:05,317 --> 00:07:10,277
Pricelist". The fields: the partner,
which is the vendor, the product,

119
00:07:10,317 --> 00:07:13,567
the price, the minimum
order quantity, and "delay".

120
00:07:14,179 --> 00:07:17,629
In Odoo, "delay" means the
delivery lead time in days.

121
00:07:18,242 --> 00:07:21,689
That's an odd name, so we explain
it in the description: "delay is the

122
00:07:21,729 --> 00:07:23,267
lead time in days."

123
00:07:23,879 --> 00:07:27,389
Without that sentence, Stocky might
think "delay" means the shipment is

124
00:07:27,429 --> 00:07:28,079
late.

125
00:07:29,275 --> 00:07:30,612
Step back and look.

126
00:07:31,225 --> 00:07:35,860
A doorbell, an intern, a brain,
a notepad, and three hands, each

127
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:37,937
reaching into a different drawer.

128
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:41,425
And notice: every tool
only uses "Get Many".

129
00:07:42,037 --> 00:07:45,275
Stocky can read, but it can't
change anything in Odoo.

130
00:07:45,887 --> 00:07:51,087
That's on purpose. Reading first,
acting later, with your approval.

131
00:07:51,700 --> 00:07:52,612
Save it.

132
00:07:54,333 --> 00:08:00,110
So, Stocky now has a chat window, a
brain, some memory and 3 tools that

133
00:08:00,150 --> 00:08:01,756
can read information from Odoo.

134
00:08:01,796 --> 00:08:03,524
But there is still one thing missing.

135
00:08:03,564 --> 00:08:05,814
It doesn't really know what its job is.

136
00:08:06,003 --> 00:08:08,855
Think about bringing a new person
into your procurement team.

137
00:08:08,895 --> 00:08:12,995
You wouldn't just sit them down and
say welcome, go and do the inventory

138
00:08:13,035 --> 00:08:16,857
stuff. You'd explain their role,
what they are responsible for,

139
00:08:17,047 --> 00:08:21,081
which system they can use, and what
they must not do, and how you expect

140
00:08:21,121 --> 00:08:22,356
them to report back.

141
00:08:22,396 --> 00:08:25,366
That's exactly what a system
prompt does for an AI agent.

142
00:08:25,676 --> 00:08:29,683
It's a job description we give
Stocky, and a good system prompt has 5

143
00:08:29,723 --> 00:08:32,724
parts. I call this the
five-part prompt anatomy.

144
00:08:32,764 --> 00:08:33,914
Let's go through them.

145
00:08:34,019 --> 00:08:36,161
First is Role. Who is Stocky?

146
00:08:36,262 --> 00:08:39,482
We tell it that it's a inventory
and procurement assistant for

147
00:08:39,522 --> 00:08:42,313
BrightLeaf Supplies working
with Sarah and her team.

148
00:08:42,353 --> 00:08:44,243
That gives the agent its context.

149
00:08:44,405 --> 00:08:47,061
Second is Goal. What are
we asking it to achieve?

150
00:08:47,101 --> 00:08:50,811
In our case, help the team
understand stock level quickly and

151
00:08:50,881 --> 00:08:52,878
accurately, so products don't run out.

152
00:08:52,918 --> 00:08:56,666
Third is tools. We explain what
each tool can access and when Stocky

153
00:08:56,706 --> 00:09:00,826
should use it. For example, if Sarah
asks about a supplier or a delivery

154
00:09:00,866 --> 00:09:04,913
lead time, Stocky should know to
use the supplier information tool.

155
00:09:05,194 --> 00:09:09,604
Fourth is rules. This is where your
business knowledge really matters.

156
00:09:09,755 --> 00:09:13,900
What exactly does running low means
in your company, for example in

157
00:09:13,940 --> 00:09:17,914
BrightLeaf, It's when the quantity
on hand is at or below the reorder

158
00:09:17,954 --> 00:09:20,123
point. And what should Stocky never do?

159
00:09:20,163 --> 00:09:22,573
Guess a number or invent a delivery date.

160
00:09:22,667 --> 00:09:24,404
And fifth is output format.

161
00:09:24,444 --> 00:09:26,253
We decide how the answer should look.

162
00:09:26,293 --> 00:09:30,907
We want a short answer first then a
table with SKU, product, quantity on

163
00:09:30,947 --> 00:09:32,956
hand, reorder point and the gap.

164
00:09:32,996 --> 00:09:36,912
We also want the result sorted by
urgency with the source shown at the

165
00:09:36,952 --> 00:09:40,390
bottom. The AI doesn't automatically
know how your company works.

166
00:09:40,430 --> 00:09:43,734
It doesn't know that your team calls
the reorder point "the red line" or

167
00:09:43,774 --> 00:09:46,857
that Mr. Harrison prefers
short practical updates.

168
00:09:46,897 --> 00:09:49,387
You know those things because
you understand the business.

169
00:09:49,485 --> 00:09:53,383
So, writing a good prompt is not
really a programming task, it's you as

170
00:09:53,423 --> 00:09:57,322
a manager explaining the job clearly
enough so that a new team member

171
00:09:57,362 --> 00:10:01,086
could do it. And that's what we
are going to put in Stocky now.

172
00:10:01,933 --> 00:10:06,393
Open the AI Agent node, go to Options,
add "System Message", delete the

173
00:10:06,433 --> 00:10:09,418
default "You are a helpful
assistant", and paste Stocky's job

174
00:10:09,458 --> 00:10:13,381
description. You'll find the full
text in the Stocky Prompt Builder

175
00:10:13,421 --> 00:10:16,496
activity file, so you don't
need to pause and type.

176
00:10:17,108 --> 00:10:20,496
Let's walk through it, because
every line is there for a reason.

177
00:10:21,703 --> 00:10:26,628
Role and Goal: who Stocky is, who it
works for, and what success means.

178
00:10:27,241 --> 00:10:30,816
Notice I wrote the headings in
capital letters with a hash sign.

179
00:10:31,428 --> 00:10:36,238
That's not code. It's just a tidy
way to organise text so both you and

180
00:10:36,278 --> 00:10:38,178
the AI can find each section.

181
00:10:39,385 --> 00:10:43,258
Tools: each tool, in plain English,
with the Odoo field names in

182
00:10:43,298 --> 00:10:47,348
brackets, so Stocky can match "lead
time" to the odd word "delay".

183
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:51,860
And one important line: "Always
use the tools to get fresh data.

184
00:10:52,473 --> 00:10:54,148
Never answer from memory."

185
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:56,448
Stock changes every hour.

186
00:10:57,060 --> 00:10:59,335
Yesterday's number is a wrong number.

187
00:11:00,523 --> 00:11:05,223
Rules. The first two are the most
important lines in the whole prompt.

188
00:11:05,836 --> 00:11:07,823
Only answer from tool data.

189
00:11:08,436 --> 00:11:11,111
And if the data isn't
there, say "I don't know."

190
00:11:11,723 --> 00:11:15,346
Then, our business definitions:
running low means at or below the

191
00:11:15,386 --> 00:11:18,846
reorder point, and the watch list
is anything within fifteen percent

192
00:11:18,886 --> 00:11:22,996
above it. That's a decision a
supply chain person makes, not a

193
00:11:23,036 --> 00:11:27,711
programmer. Change the fifteen
percent to whatever your company uses.

194
00:11:28,909 --> 00:11:32,807
And the output format: a one-line
answer first, then a table,

195
00:11:32,847 --> 00:11:35,922
sorted by urgency, and a
source line at the end.

196
00:11:36,534 --> 00:11:38,759
The source line is my favourite trick.

197
00:11:39,372 --> 00:11:43,057
It forces Stocky to tell you which
drawers it opened, so you can always

198
00:11:43,097 --> 00:11:43,772
check.

199
00:11:44,959 --> 00:11:49,359
Pause and think. What is your
company's definition of running low?

200
00:11:49,972 --> 00:11:53,519
Write it down as one sentence a new
intern could follow without asking

201
00:11:53,559 --> 00:11:54,959
you a single question.

202
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:06,550
Here we go. The question
Sarah asks every morning.

203
00:12:07,163 --> 00:12:08,413
"What's running low?"

204
00:12:09,025 --> 00:12:11,850
Press Enter. Watch the canvas.

205
00:12:12,463 --> 00:12:16,575
The brain lights up, then the stock
tool, then the reorder rules tool.

206
00:12:17,188 --> 00:12:19,213
Stocky is opening the drawers.

207
00:12:19,825 --> 00:12:21,238
And here's the answer.

208
00:12:21,850 --> 00:12:25,250
Four products running low, and
one more on the watch list.

209
00:12:25,863 --> 00:12:29,300
Safety gloves, four hundred
and twenty against six hundred.

210
00:12:29,913 --> 00:12:32,975
Pallet jack wheels, eighteen
against twenty-five.

211
00:12:33,588 --> 00:12:36,300
Hydraulic oil, thirty-one against forty.

212
00:12:36,913 --> 00:12:40,773
And shipping boxes, one thousand one
hundred and eighty against fifteen

213
00:12:40,813 --> 00:12:41,538
hundred.

214
00:12:42,742 --> 00:12:47,302
Look at the order. The boxes have
the biggest gap in units, minus three

215
00:12:47,342 --> 00:12:49,667
hundred and twenty, but
they're at the bottom.

216
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:54,917
Why? Because we told Stocky to
sort by percentage shortfall.

217
00:12:55,530 --> 00:12:59,065
Gloves are thirty percent below
their reorder point, boxes only

218
00:12:59,104 --> 00:13:00,517
twenty-one percent.

219
00:13:01,129 --> 00:13:03,655
That's the rule we
wrote, followed exactly.

220
00:13:04,267 --> 00:13:06,917
It even flagged the LED
lights on the watch list.

221
00:13:07,530 --> 00:13:09,280
Thirty-four against thirty.

222
00:13:09,892 --> 00:13:13,202
And with a twenty-eight-day lead time
from Shenzhen, that's exactly the

223
00:13:13,242 --> 00:13:15,392
one Sarah would want to know about.

224
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:18,013
But don't trust it yet.

225
00:13:18,625 --> 00:13:21,025
Check it. Here's Odoo.

226
00:13:21,638 --> 00:13:23,638
Gloves, four hundred and twenty.

227
00:13:24,250 --> 00:13:27,775
Wheels, eighteen. The numbers match.

228
00:13:28,388 --> 00:13:30,738
Ten seconds instead of forty minutes.

229
00:13:31,930 --> 00:13:36,877
Second question: "Which supplier
provides BL-1005, and what's the lead

230
00:13:36,917 --> 00:13:39,892
time?" Global Lift Parts.

231
00:13:40,505 --> 00:13:45,743
Twenty-one days. Thirty-eight dollars
a set, minimum order ten sets.

232
00:13:46,355 --> 00:13:50,027
And without being asked, it connected
the dots: eighteen on hand against

233
00:13:50,067 --> 00:13:54,203
a reorder point of twenty-five, and
a new order takes twenty-one days,

234
00:13:54,243 --> 00:13:56,780
so stock could run
short before it arrives.

235
00:13:57,392 --> 00:13:59,780
That's the kind of thinking
a good intern does.

236
00:14:00,988 --> 00:14:02,363
Now watch the memory.

237
00:14:02,975 --> 00:14:05,788
I'll just say, "And what
about the hydraulic oil?"

238
00:14:06,401 --> 00:14:08,863
I didn't say "supplier" or "lead time".

239
00:14:09,475 --> 00:14:12,486
But Stocky remembered what we were
talking about, thanks to Simple

240
00:14:12,526 --> 00:14:16,063
Memory, and gave me Gulf
Lubricants, fourteen days.

241
00:14:16,676 --> 00:14:18,500
That's the notepad at work.

242
00:14:19,702 --> 00:14:24,387
Pause and think. If you were Sarah,
which product would you order first,

243
00:14:24,427 --> 00:14:28,477
and why? Hint: compare the
gap with the lead time.

244
00:14:36,033 --> 00:14:38,358
So far we've seen what Stocky said.

245
00:14:38,971 --> 00:14:40,758
Now let's see how it thought.

246
00:14:41,371 --> 00:14:44,096
Open the "Logs" panel at
the bottom of the canvas.

247
00:14:44,708 --> 00:14:47,621
This is the step-by-step
story of the last question.

248
00:14:48,233 --> 00:14:50,496
Read it from top to bottom like a diary.

249
00:14:51,699 --> 00:14:55,322
Step one: the brain reads the
question and our job description,

250
00:14:55,362 --> 00:14:58,899
and decides, "To answer this,
I need the supplier info."

251
00:14:59,512 --> 00:15:01,049
It doesn't answer yet.

252
00:15:01,662 --> 00:15:05,772
It asks for tools. That's the big
difference between an agent and a

253
00:15:05,812 --> 00:15:10,587
chatbot. A chatbot answers straight
away from what it already knows.

254
00:15:11,199 --> 00:15:14,199
An agent first decides
what it needs to find out.

255
00:15:15,405 --> 00:15:17,955
Step two: the Odoo tool runs.

256
00:15:18,568 --> 00:15:21,430
Click it and you can see
exactly what Odoo sent back.

257
00:15:22,043 --> 00:15:23,893
I'll switch to JSON view.

258
00:15:24,505 --> 00:15:26,693
Remember JSON from Video 3?

259
00:15:27,305 --> 00:15:28,980
A labelled shopping list.

260
00:15:29,593 --> 00:15:31,880
Partner: Global Lift Parts.

261
00:15:32,493 --> 00:15:36,668
Product: BL-1005, Pallet Jack Wheels.

262
00:15:37,280 --> 00:15:38,993
Price: thirty-eight.

263
00:15:39,605 --> 00:15:41,568
Minimum quantity: ten.

264
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:46,878
Delay: twenty-one. Every number in
Stocky's answer is right here in the

265
00:15:46,918 --> 00:15:49,980
shopping list. Nothing was made up.

266
00:15:51,183 --> 00:15:54,868
Step three: the brain reads the
shopping lists and writes the answer in

267
00:15:54,908 --> 00:15:59,618
our format. So the pattern is
always: think, use a tool, read the

268
00:15:59,658 --> 00:16:01,346
result, then answer.

269
00:16:01,958 --> 00:16:07,096
Sometimes it loops, think, tool,
think, another tool, before answering.

270
00:16:07,708 --> 00:16:09,883
That loop is what makes it an agent.

271
00:16:11,073 --> 00:16:15,058
And every conversation is also saved
in the Executions tab, our logbook

272
00:16:15,098 --> 00:16:17,533
from Video 5. If Mr.

273
00:16:17,573 --> 00:16:21,108
Harrison ever asks, "Where did
Stocky get that number on Tuesday?",

274
00:16:21,148 --> 00:16:24,573
you can open the run and show
him the exact data Odoo returned.

275
00:16:25,186 --> 00:16:26,511
That's traceability.

276
00:16:27,123 --> 00:16:29,561
Supply chain people love traceability.

277
00:16:33,695 --> 00:16:37,555
Now, I want to talk about one of the
biggest risks when you start using

278
00:16:37,595 --> 00:16:39,376
AI in business. Hallucination.

279
00:16:39,424 --> 00:16:41,394
Let me explain it with a quick story.

280
00:16:41,664 --> 00:16:44,600
Imagine there is a new intern at
BrightLeaf, let's call him Jake.

281
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:48,500
Jake is smart, he is keen to learn
and he really wants to make a good

282
00:16:48,540 --> 00:16:50,226
impression. One day, Mr.

283
00:16:50,266 --> 00:16:54,775
Harrison walks over and asks him,
When are the LED warehouse lights from

284
00:16:54,815 --> 00:16:56,172
Shenzhen arriving?

285
00:16:58,057 --> 00:16:59,371
Jake doesn't actually know.

286
00:16:59,411 --> 00:17:02,691
He hasn't checked the purchase order
and he doesn't have a confirmed

287
00:17:02,731 --> 00:17:05,752
delivery date. But he also
doesn't want to disappoint Mr.

288
00:17:05,792 --> 00:17:08,741
Harrison. So he says, Tuesday, sir.

289
00:17:10,947 --> 00:17:12,357
It sounds confident.

290
00:17:12,567 --> 00:17:16,267
Mr. Harrison trusts the answer
and promises a customer that the

291
00:17:16,307 --> 00:17:17,857
installation will happen on Wednesday.

292
00:17:17,926 --> 00:17:19,076
But there's a problem.

293
00:17:19,117 --> 00:17:21,307
The lights have not even left the factory.

294
00:17:21,462 --> 00:17:23,772
The supplier's lead time is 28 days.

295
00:17:23,812 --> 00:17:25,828
Jake was not trying to mislead anyone.

296
00:17:25,868 --> 00:17:29,624
He simply gave an answer that sounds
plausible, even though he didn't

297
00:17:29,664 --> 00:17:33,610
have the facts. That's very similar
to what an AI hallucination looks

298
00:17:33,695 --> 00:17:37,931
like. Models like Claude and ChatGPT
generate response by predicting

299
00:17:38,081 --> 00:17:40,154
what words are likely to come next.

300
00:17:40,194 --> 00:17:43,876
Most of the time that produces useful
answers, but when the information

301
00:17:43,916 --> 00:17:47,969
is not available the model can still
produce a very convincing response.

302
00:17:48,009 --> 00:17:52,169
It might give you a delivery date,
a stock quantity or even a supplier

303
00:17:52,209 --> 00:17:56,425
name. And it can sound just as
confident as it does when the answer is

304
00:17:56,465 --> 00:17:59,624
correct. In supply chain that
can be a serious problem.

305
00:17:59,672 --> 00:18:03,006
A made-up delivery date can become
a broken promise to a customer.

306
00:18:03,046 --> 00:18:06,804
A made-up stock number can lead to
a stock-out or an unnecessary rush

307
00:18:06,844 --> 00:18:09,689
order. So how do we reduce
that risk with Stocky?

308
00:18:09,729 --> 00:18:11,739
There are three habits we want to build.

309
00:18:11,900 --> 00:18:15,690
First, give the agent access to the
real information through its tools.

310
00:18:15,889 --> 00:18:19,999
Second, make it clear in the instruction
that it can only answer using

311
00:18:20,039 --> 00:18:21,919
the data those tools return.

312
00:18:22,123 --> 00:18:26,343
And third, make it completely
acceptable for Stocky to say "I don't

313
00:18:26,383 --> 00:18:30,674
know". An agent that admits it doesn't
have the information is much more

314
00:18:30,714 --> 00:18:33,253
useful than the one that
confidently makes something up.

315
00:18:33,293 --> 00:18:34,663
Let's put that to test.

316
00:18:34,953 --> 00:18:38,293
We will deliberately give Stocky
a weak instruction, see what it

317
00:18:38,333 --> 00:18:39,683
invents, then fix it.

318
00:18:40,633 --> 00:18:44,243
For this experiment, I'll temporarily
replace Stocky's job description

319
00:18:44,283 --> 00:18:47,531
with a lazy one-liner: "You are
a helpful inventory assistant for

320
00:18:47,571 --> 00:18:48,983
BrightLeaf Supplies."

321
00:18:49,596 --> 00:18:51,296
That's what most people write.

322
00:18:51,908 --> 00:18:53,871
I've saved the good version safely.

323
00:18:54,483 --> 00:18:56,358
Now I'll start a fresh chat.

324
00:18:57,547 --> 00:19:01,145
Now a question Stocky cannot answer,
because it has no tool for purchase

325
00:19:01,185 --> 00:19:06,257
orders. "When will purchase order P
zero zero zero two seven from Global

326
00:19:06,297 --> 00:19:07,697
Lift Parts arrive?"

327
00:19:08,310 --> 00:19:12,907
And look. "Expected to arrive in
about three weeks, around the end of

328
00:19:12,947 --> 00:19:15,710
the month." It sounds great.

329
00:19:16,322 --> 00:19:17,935
It's completely made up.

330
00:19:18,547 --> 00:19:20,885
Stocky never saw that purchase order.

331
00:19:21,497 --> 00:19:25,922
In Video 5, our email said that
order was already three days late!

332
00:19:26,535 --> 00:19:29,347
It took the lead time and
invented a delivery date.

333
00:19:29,960 --> 00:19:32,285
That's Jake saying "Tuesday, sir."

334
00:19:33,469 --> 00:19:36,044
And here's how you catch it: open the log.

335
00:19:36,657 --> 00:19:38,894
There's no purchase order data anywhere.

336
00:19:39,507 --> 00:19:41,119
Only supplier info.

337
00:19:41,732 --> 00:19:44,732
The answer can't be traced,
so it can't be trusted.

338
00:19:45,919 --> 00:19:50,167
Now let's put the real job description
back, reset the chat, and ask the

339
00:19:50,207 --> 00:19:51,619
exact same question.

340
00:19:52,232 --> 00:19:56,669
"I don't know. That information
isn't in the Odoo data I can see."

341
00:19:57,282 --> 00:20:01,317
It explains what it CAN see, gives
the useful facts it has, the standard

342
00:20:01,357 --> 00:20:05,182
lead times, clearly labelled as
defaults, not a promised date.

343
00:20:05,794 --> 00:20:09,807
And it tells Sarah exactly where
to check: Purchase, Orders.

344
00:20:10,419 --> 00:20:12,069
That's the intern you want.

345
00:20:13,273 --> 00:20:18,286
Two more traps. A product
that doesn't exist, BL-1011.

346
00:20:18,898 --> 00:20:23,148
Stocky says it can't find it, and
tells me which SKUs it can see.

347
00:20:23,761 --> 00:20:27,033
And an action it isn't allowed to
take: "Order fifty more pallet jack

348
00:20:27,073 --> 00:20:30,498
wheels." Stocky says it can only read.

349
00:20:31,111 --> 00:20:35,333
Good. Ordering is next video, and
it will always come to you for

350
00:20:35,373 --> 00:20:36,636
approval first.

351
00:20:44,400 --> 00:20:46,804
Take a moment and look
at what you have built.

352
00:20:46,844 --> 00:20:50,374
Stocky can now connect to Odoo,
read live inventory information,

353
00:20:50,488 --> 00:20:54,034
compare stock against the reorder
points, look up supplier details,

354
00:20:54,124 --> 00:20:56,054
and answer questions in plain English.

355
00:20:56,145 --> 00:21:00,271
And just as importantly, it can show
us where its answer came from and

356
00:21:00,311 --> 00:21:02,377
admit when it doesn't
have enough information.

357
00:21:02,417 --> 00:21:05,267
We did all of that without
writing a single line of code.

358
00:21:05,321 --> 00:21:08,741
Before we move on, there are three
things I want you to remember from

359
00:21:08,781 --> 00:21:12,830
this video. First, an AI agent
needs more than just a brain.

360
00:21:12,987 --> 00:21:16,882
Stocky has a chat trigger to receive
questions, Claude as its brain,

361
00:21:17,061 --> 00:21:20,801
simple memory to follow the conversation,
and three Odoo tools to read

362
00:21:20,841 --> 00:21:25,200
the data. Each tool needs a clear
name, a clear description, and only

363
00:21:25,240 --> 00:21:26,910
the fields it actually needs.

364
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:30,410
Second, the system prompt is
the agent's job description.

365
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:35,591
Remember those five parts, role,
goal, tools, rules and output format.

366
00:21:35,631 --> 00:21:39,431
The rules are especially important
because that's where you define how

367
00:21:39,471 --> 00:21:40,509
your business works.

368
00:21:40,549 --> 00:21:44,639
For example, what does running
low actually mean in your company?

369
00:21:44,754 --> 00:21:49,446
And third, always trust but verify,
check the execution log, compare

370
00:21:49,486 --> 00:21:52,580
important numbers with Odoo, and test
the agent with questions it should

371
00:21:52,620 --> 00:21:53,831
not be able to answer.

372
00:21:53,871 --> 00:21:57,788
If the information is not available,
Stocky should say so rather than

373
00:21:57,828 --> 00:22:01,338
guess. Now let's put what we
have learned into practice.

374
00:22:01,533 --> 00:22:05,889
First, open the V06 Activity:
Stocky Prompt Builder document.

375
00:22:05,966 --> 00:22:10,006
It includes Stocky's prompt as an
example along with the space for you

376
00:22:10,046 --> 00:22:11,987
to create the one for your own company.

377
00:22:12,027 --> 00:22:15,037
Make sure you define what "running
low" means in your business.

378
00:22:15,300 --> 00:22:20,606
Next, open the V06 Activity: test
question bank spreadsheet, ask Stocky

379
00:22:20,646 --> 00:22:25,578
all 20 questions, mark each answer
as a pass or fail and let the sheet

380
00:22:25,728 --> 00:22:27,098
calculate its pass rate.

381
00:22:27,330 --> 00:22:32,153
Aim for at least 18 correct answer
out of 20, then take the Video 6

382
00:22:32,193 --> 00:22:34,924
quiz. And here's where
things get interesting.

383
00:22:35,016 --> 00:22:39,530
Right now, Stocky can read information
from your ERP, but it can't take

384
00:22:39,570 --> 00:22:42,752
action. Sarah still has to create
the purchase order herself.

385
00:22:43,142 --> 00:22:46,762
In the next video, we will give
Stocky the ability to prepare the

386
00:22:46,802 --> 00:22:50,447
purchase order in Odoo, but it
won't simply send it on its own.

387
00:22:50,525 --> 00:22:53,995
It will stop and ask for human
approval before anything happens.

388
00:22:54,233 --> 00:22:58,313
That's the next step, moving from
an agent that can read to an agent

389
00:22:58,353 --> 00:22:59,943
that can act responsibly.

390
00:23:00,411 --> 00:23:04,868
Video 7, Agents That Take action,
draft purchase orders with human

391
00:23:04,908 --> 00:23:06,411
approval. I'll see you there.
